        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 776,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Lists each different value in an array once, in increasing order, with how many times it occurs - and, for every element of the input, which of those values it is.\n\nUse it to find which labels, notes, states or zones turned up in a stream of them and how often; to count votes or classifications; or, with the inverse, to turn arbitrary values (MIDI notes 60 64 67) into consecutive numbers (0 1 2) that can index a list, a colour table or a set of outlets.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.unique\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every element is counted, whatever the shape. Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalues:\nThe distinct values, smallest first.\n\ncounts:\nHow many times each of them occurs.\n\ninverse:\nFor each element of the input, the position of its value in values - the input re-labelled 0, 1, 2 ... It has the input's shape. Sent first, then counts, then values.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n3 1 3 2 3 holds three different values, 1 2 3. 1 and 2 occur once each and 3 three times. The inverse, 2 0 2 1 2, is the input rewritten as positions in 1 2 3: the 3s became 2, the 1 became 0. A metro re-sends the input five times a second; edit it and click.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.histogram counts values falling into ranges rather than exact values. np.sort puts every value in order, repeats and all. np.take turns positions back into values.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Lists each different value in an array once, in increasing order, with how many times it occurs - and, for every element of the input, which of those values it is.\n\nUse it to find which labels, notes, states or zones turned up in a stream of them and how often; to count votes or classifications; or, with the inverse, to turn arbitrary values (MIDI notes 60 64 67) into consecutive numbers (0 1 2) that can index a list, a colour table or a set of outlets.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.unique\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every element is counted, whatever the shape. Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalues:\nThe distinct values, smallest first.\n\ncounts:\nHow many times each of them occurs.\n\ninverse:\nFor each element of the input, the position of its value in values - the input re-labelled 0, 1, 2 ... It has the input's shape. Sent first, then counts, then values.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n3 1 3 2 3 holds three different values, 1 2 3. 1 and 2 occur once each and 3 three times. The inverse, 2 0 2 1 2, is the input rewritten as positions in 1 2 3: the 3s became 2, the 1 became 0. A metro re-sends the input five times a second; edit it and click.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.histogram counts values falling into ranges rather than exact values. np.sort puts every value in order, repeats and all. np.take turns positions back into values."
        },
